# PRD-038 Research Report: Multi-Repository Have-At-It Acceptance

## 1. What `crates/familiar-ai-mcp` does

`crates/familiar-ai-mcp` is the **MCP (Model Context Protocol) server** that exposes Familiar's context/stewardship/control-plane surface to coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, etc.) over stdio JSON-RPC.

- `crates/familiar-ai-mcp/src/lib.rs:1-4` — doc comment: "Implements a JSON-RPC 2.0 server over Content-Length-framed stdio, targeting MCP protocol version 2024-11-05."
- Public modules (`crates/familiar-ai-mcp/src/lib.rs:6-18`): `handshake`, `meta`, `protocol`, `server`, `storage`, `tool`, `tools`, `transport`. Re-exports: `McpServer`, `SqliteStorage`/`Storage`/`StorageError`, `Tool`/`ToolContext`/`ToolError`/`ToolRegistry`, `StdioTransport`/`Transport`/`TransportEvent`.
- Binary: `crates/familiar-ai-mcp/src/bin/familiar-ai-mcp.rs`. `main()`/`run()` (lines 14-109): resolves `AppPaths`, loads `Config`, inits stderr-only logging, reads a daemon-issued capability session credential from `FAMILIAR_AI_MCP_SESSION_FILE`, connects via `familiar_ai_daemon::local_transport::LocalClient` to the daemon's control-plane Unix socket using `ClientHello`, builds `ToolContext { storage: Arc::new(UnavailableStorage), status, config, router: None }`, registers only the `control_plane` tools (`control.get_assignment`, `control.report_progress`, `control.submit_evidence`, `control.request_escalation`) — note: the *agent-facing* binary registers **only** control-plane tools, not the full `register_default_tools` set (that's registered in `crates/familiar-ai-mcp/src/tools/mod.rs:29` and is exercised by the test suite via a different composition path, likely a daemon-hosted/dashboard-facing variant not shown in the binary above — worth confirming during implementation which binary/composition root actually wires `register_default_tools`).
- Key types: `ToolContext` (`crates/familiar-ai-mcp/src/tool.rs:24-34`: `storage: Arc<dyn Storage>`, `status: Arc<Mutex<AppStatus>>`, `config: Arc<Config>`, `router: Option<Arc<InferenceRouter>>`), `Tool` trait (`tool.rs:36-42`: `name`, `description`, `input_schema`, `async fn call(&self, args: Value, ctx: &ToolContext) -> Result<Value, ToolError>`), `ToolRegistry`, `McpServer`, `Storage`/`SqliteStorage`/`UnavailableStorage` (`src/storage.rs`), `StdioTransport`/`MockTransport` (`src/transport.rs`).
- Tool modules (`crates/familiar-ai-mcp/src/tools/mod.rs:1-19`): `control_plane`, `create_decision`, `get_file_summary`, `get_module_summary`, `get_project_status`, `get_recent_changes`, `get_recent_decisions`, `get_session_rollups`, `keywords`, `pack_for_task`, `remember_result`, `repository_map`, `scoring`, `search`, `session_rollup_query`, `stewardship_mutations`, `stewardship_reads`, `stewardship_support`, `usage_series`. `register_default_tools()` (`tools/mod.rs:29-55`) registers all 25 (context + stewardship read/mutation) tools.

## 2. Existing tests under `crates/familiar-ai-mcp/tests/`

Two files:

- **`crates/familiar-ai-mcp/tests/integration.rs`** (370 lines) — full JSON-RPC round-trip tests against an in-memory `Storage`/`Database`. Pattern (`build_server`, lines 14-56): `Database::open_in_memory()` + `run_migrations()`, seed one project via `NewProject`, wrap in `SqliteStorage`, build `ToolContext` with `Config::default()` and `router: None`, `register_default_tools(&mut registry)`, drive it with `MockTransport::new(inbound: Vec<String>)` which replays a scripted sequence of raw JSON-RPC request strings and records `TransportEvent::Write` responses (`events_handle()`). Tests cover: `full_session_handshake_list_call`, `remember_result_then_get_recent_decisions_via_repo_root`, `search_returns_results`, `version_mismatch_returns_structured_error`, `parse_error_response`, `method_before_initialize_errors`, `unknown_method_returns_method_not_found`. **This is the reusable pattern for exercising the MCP surface deterministically without a real subprocess** — no fake external agent needed since these tools only touch storage/config, not a coding agent.

- **`crates/familiar-ai-mcp/tests/stewardship.rs`** (462 lines) — "PRD-035 stewardship tool coverage... exercised against a real temp Git repository and SQLite database, the same fixture shape the `familiar-ai` CLI tests use." Key helper `repo_fixture()` (lines 33-60): `tempfile::tempdir()`, `git init -q`, writes `docs/prds/PRD-1.md`/`PRD-2.md`, resolves `RepositoryIdentity` via `FilesystemBacklogDiscovery`, opens a real `Database` at `state.db` inside the temp repo, runs migrations, reconciles the backlog via `SqliteBacklogRepository::reconcile_and_snapshot`. Then calls `stewardship_reads`/`stewardship_mutations` tools directly (not through the JSON-RPC transport) with a `ToolContext` built around that DB/repo. **This is the closest existing pattern to what a multi-repo acceptance test needs**: real temp git repo + real SQLite DB + real backlog discovery/reconciliation, called twice with different fixture shapes would directly generalize to "two materially different repositories."

- `crates/familiar-ai-mcp/src/tools/control_plane.rs:113-209` (unit test module inside `control_plane.rs`, not a `tests/` file) shows the pattern for exercising the **daemon control-plane boundary from the MCP side**: spins up a real `familiar_ai_daemon::control_plane::ControlPlaneService` over an in-memory `Database`, registers a project, submits/claims/binds a worker, mints a session credential (`mint_worker_session`), binds a `LocalHost` Unix-socket listener, connects a `LocalClient`, registers the `control_plane` tools, and asserts the model-facing tool surface can report progress / request escalation but **has no approval tool** (`assert!(!registry.list().iter().any(|t| t.name.contains("approve")))`). This is a strong template for proving "worker capability separation" from the MCP side.

- `crates/familiar-ai-mcp/src/tools/test_helpers.rs` (`#[cfg(test)]` module, `tools/mod.rs:21-22`) — `DummyStorage` (all-`Ok(empty)`/`Err(dummy)` fake `Storage` impl) and `dummy_ctx()` helper for unit tests that don't need real state.

No existing MCP test spins up a fake *coding agent* — all MCP tests only exercise storage/backlog/control-plane; agent execution ("cheap-or-local task" / "independent strong review") is tested on the **daemon** side (see §3/§4).

## 3. Worker routing / probation and empirical routing (PRD-032)

Two layers:

**a) Deterministic routing engine** — `crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/registry.rs` (663 lines). Adapter-neutral capability routing, fully unit-testable with fakes and no I/O:
- `WorkerStage` enum (Planning/Implementation/Review/Remediation/NarrowTask) and `WorkerCapability` (registry.rs:8-24).
- `WorkerDescriptor` struct (registry.rs:39-55): `id, spec_identity, empirical_version, runtime_id, provider, model, executable, capabilities, fresh_process_isolation, context_tokens, estimated_cost_microusd, available, effort, permission_mode, extra_args`.
- `RouteRequest` (registry.rs:137-146): `stage, pinned_worker, max_cost_microusd, required_context_tokens, require_isolation, independent_from: Option<(provider, model)>, risk_classes, expected_file_count`.
- `RouteRule` (registry.rs:154-173): operator rule, first-match-wins, matches on risk-class intersection and/or `max_expected_files` ceiling — this is exactly the "cheap-or-local task" routing knob.
- `WorkerRegistry::select(&self, request: &RouteRequest) -> Result<SelectionRecord, RouteError>` (registry.rs:306-411): evaluates every candidate, rejects for `Unavailable/OverBudget/Incapable/InsufficientContext/NoIsolation/NotIndependent/NotPinned/NotRouted`, applies route rules (unpinned only), else falls back to lowest-cost-then-id. **Independence enforcement**: `request.independent_from` rejects a reviewer whose `(provider, model)` matches the implementer's; if no reviewer remains, `RouteError::NoIndependentReviewer` (registry.rs:336-339, 383-385). Existing unit tests directly demonstrate the pattern for a deterministic test: `reviewer_must_be_independent` (line 482), `risk_class_rule_selects_before_size_rule_and_cost_tiebreak` (line 504, proves a cheap worker vs. a "careful"/strong worker chosen by risk class), `unmatched_rules_fall_back_to_lowest_cost_then_id` (line 539), `pinned_worker_bypasses_route_rules` (line 580).

- Daemon composition root: `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/run.rs`. `RouteContext { risk_classes: Vec<String>, expected_file_count: u64 }` (run.rs:390-394) is derived per-PRD by `route_context_for_prd()` (run.rs:400-412) from structured PRD front matter. `resolved_worker_plan(config: &Config, route_context: &RouteContext) -> Result<(AgentEntryConfig, AgentEntryConfig, Vec<SelectionRecord>), String>` (run.rs:416-502) builds a `WorkerRegistry` from `config.worker_registry`, selects Implementation, Remediation, and (if `config.review.enabled`) an independent Review worker (`independent_from: Some((implementation_worker.provider, implementation_worker.model))`, `require_isolation: stage == WorkerStage::Review`). This is the single function a multi-repo acceptance test should call/exercise to prove "routing includes a cheap-or-local task and an independent strong review" — feed it a `Config` with a `worker_registry` containing a cheap/local worker and a stronger reviewer worker plus a `WorkerRouteRuleConfig`, and assert `records` shows the cheap worker for Implementation and a *different* provider/model for Review.
- `next_implementation_worker()` (run.rs:507-538) — escalation to the next more expensive eligible worker (used on repeated failure, not needed for baseline acceptance but relevant to "injected failures" AC).
- Selections are persisted via `familiar_ai_storage::WorkerSelectionRepository::record(...)` (`crates/familiar-ai-storage/src/repos/worker_selection.rs:21-28`, table `worker_selections`, migration `020_worker_selections.sql` + `023_worker_selection_routing_inputs.sql`) — call sites in `run.rs` around line 1210-1301.

**b) Probation/empirical scoring (PRD-032 proper)** — `crates/familiar-ai-core/src/probation.rs` (135 lines): `WorkerStanding` enum (Probation/Promoted/Quarantined/Retired), `ProbationPolicy` struct (`policy_id, version, minimum_accepted_prds, minimum_review_pass_basis_points, maximum_remediation_basis_points, maximum_failure_basis_points, probation_max_expected_files, require_independent_review`), `EmpiricalMetrics`, `EmpiricalScore`, and pure function `score(policy, metrics, trusted_identity) -> EmpiricalScore` (probation.rs:68-102) which computes `promotion_eligible` and refuses to treat unknown identity/cost as cheap/trusted (unit test `unknown_identity_and_cost_never_become_trusted_or_cheap`, line 108). Persistence: `crates/familiar-ai-storage/src/repos/probation.rs` (`ProbationRepository`, referenced from `run.rs:1264` and `1914`), migration `045_model_probation.sql`. Daemon glue: `run.rs::worker_probation_policy()` (line 1879) and `persist_probation_outcome()` (line 1893) record a `ProbationObservation` with `independent_review_passed: Option<bool>` per stage per execution — this is the durable evidence a multi-repo acceptance test can query after a drive session to prove independent review actually happened for each repo.

## 4. Independent review / separation-of-duties (PRD-033)

Two distinct mechanisms live in two different crates:

**a) Review-cycle coordination (implementer vs. independent reviewer within one execution)** — `crates/familiar-ai-review` (7 files, `lib.rs` re-exports everything: `agent_adapter`, `coordinator`, `evidence`, `expected_files`, `package`, `policy`, `tier`, `types`, `verification`). `coordinator.rs` (1987 lines) is the review-cycle state machine; its test module defines fake `RemediationAgent` implementers (`Implementer`, `SuccessfulImplementer`, `FailingImplementer` — lines 1097, 1170, 1487) as the pattern for a fake agent. `tier.rs` implements cost-tier review policy (checks-only/standard/full — `ReviewTierConfig` in `config/review.rs:104-109`), and `review_routing.rs` daemon test (`crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/review_routing.rs`) exercises `select_capability_proven_reviewer`/`validate_review_capability` (from `familiar_ai_review`) to prove an incapable reviewer is quarantined and rerouted — a good template for asserting review-capability gating deterministically with fakes (`ReviewCapabilityProbe`, `ReviewCapabilityReason`).

`config/review.rs:8-45` (`ReviewConfig`): `enabled, max_review_attempts, max_remediation_attempts, allow_isolated_same_model_fallback, implementation_agent: ReviewAgentConfig, reviewer_agent: ReviewAgentConfig, tier_policy: Option<ReviewTierPolicyConfig>` (`ReviewAgentConfig { adapter_id, agent_id, provider: Option<String>, model: Option<String> }`, lines 47-53). `run.rs` cross-checks that the resolved implementation/reviewer identities line up with these configured identities (search for `config.review.implementation_agent`/`reviewer_agent` around `run.rs:1444, 3631-3637`).

**b) Repository-owned delivery approval separation (PRD-033's implementer/reviewer/approver triple)** — `crates/familiar-ai-core/src/config/delivery.rs`:
- `DeliveryMode` enum (lines 53-59): `Disabled, ReviewedPrManual, PocSelfApproval, ReviewGatedAutomatic`. Default is `ReviewedPrManual` (`default_delivery_mode()`, line 61-63).
- `ReviewGateConfig` struct (lines 187-193): exactly `{ implementer: String, reviewer: String, approver: String }` — this **is** the separation-of-duties surface named in PRD-033 AC4/AC9.
- `PocSelfApprovalWarrant` struct (lines 174-181): `actor, max_prds, expires_at, assurance_label` (fixed default `"LOW_ASSURANCE_POC_SELF_APPROVAL"`).
- `DeliveryConfig::validate()` (lines 208-289) is where separation is **enforced**: for `ReviewGatedAutomatic`, lines 276-286 require `gate.implementer`, `gate.reviewer`, `gate.approver` all non-empty **and pairwise distinct** — `"review-gated delivery requires three distinct non-empty implementer, reviewer, and approver identities"`. For `PocSelfApproval`, requires `actor` to start with `"human:"`, finite `max_prds`, valid RFC3339 `expires_at`, the fixed low-assurance label, and prohibits `staging_environment` of `"production"`/`"prod"` (lines 249-273).
- Persisted evidence: `crates/familiar-ai-storage/src/repos/delivery.rs` — `DeliveryAuthorityDecision`/`DeliveryDecisionRow` structs (lines 22-50: `prd_id, mode, actor, decision, assurance_label, findings_json, stop_reasons_json, warrant_consumed`), `DeliveryRepository::record_authority_decision(...)` (lines 62-79), table populated via migration `021_delivery_policy.sql`/`052_autonomous_delivery_decisions.sql`. This is the durable row a multi-repo acceptance test should query to prove "manual delivery stops at reviewed PR" vs. "PoC mode self-approves only within its warrant" vs. "review-gated mode preserves separation evidence" (PRD-038 AC6).
- Daemon runtime: `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/delivery.rs` — `execute_configured()` (lines 34-113) is the top-level delivery entry point reading `repository_config.delivery_policy()`; `deliver()`/`deliver_with()`/`deliver_to_with()` (lines 209, 217, 442) implement the actual publish/merge/deploy flow per mode. Test fixture at line 918-928 constructs a `ReviewGateConfig { implementer: "impl", reviewer: ..., approver: "approve" }` inline — useful as a literal template.
- Existing daemon test `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/autonomous_delivery.rs` (340 lines) is the closest existing single-repo test of the full delivery-mode/warrant machinery and should be read in full before writing the daemon-side acceptance test (not fully excerpted here for brevity, but it uses the same `fixture()`-style temp-repo + `Config` + fake delivery adapter pattern as `drive_loop.rs`).

## 5. `familiar-ai-core` config — exact structs/fields/defaults

Note: there is no single `config.rs` file; it's a `config/` directory (`crates/familiar-ai-core/src/config/mod.rs` plus per-topic files). Top-level `Config` struct: `crates/familiar-ai-core/src/config/mod.rs:55-122`.

**Repositories** (`config/mod.rs:56-61`, struct in `config/repository.rs`):
```rust
pub repositories_dir: Option<PathBuf>,              // config/mod.rs:59
pub repositories: BTreeMap<String, RepositoryConfig>, // config/mod.rs:61, keyed by absolute worktree path
```
`RepositoryConfig` (`config/repository.rs:9-40`, `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]`): `profile` (default `"canonical"`), `active_dir` (default `"docs/prds"`), `archived_dir` (default `"docs/prds/done"`), `prd_metadata_policy` (default `"incremental"`), `reference_roots: Vec<ReferenceRootConfig>`, `risk_vocabulary: Vec<String>`, `review: Option<ReviewConfig>`, `execution_context: Option<ExecutionContextConfig>`, `delivery: Option<DeliveryConfig>` (absence = fail-closed, no publish authority — `delivery_policy()` at repository.rs:92-96), `bindings: BTreeMap<String, String>`. Defaults shown in `config/default.toml:50-94` as commented `[repositories."/absolute/path/to/worktree"]` example, including a full per-repo override worked example (execution_context, review, review.scope, review.verification, review.implementation_agent/reviewer_agent).

**Delivery mode selection** — `config/delivery.rs:8-51` `DeliveryConfig` (global default table `[delivery]`, per-repo override under `repositories.*.delivery`): `mode: DeliveryMode` (default `ReviewedPrManual`), `enabled` (legacy compat, never grants authority alone), `max_deliveries_per_session`, `command_timeout_ms` (default 1_800_000), `remote`, `base`, `provider_argv`, `auto_merge` (legacy), `staging_environment`, `deploy_argv`, `smoke_argv`, `rollback_argv`, `comment_blockers`, `required_checks`, `migration_gate_argv`, `credential_references`, `poc_warrant: Option<PocSelfApprovalWarrant>`, `review_gate: Option<ReviewGateConfig>`, `targets: BTreeMap<role, target>`. Default in `config/default.toml`: no `[delivery]` section is uncommented at top level; the `[driver]` section comment block (lines 117-140) documents the per-repository `delivery` section shape operators must add.

**Worker/review agent assignment** — `config/review.rs:8-45` `ReviewConfig` (global default `[review]` section, `config/default.toml:160-171`: `enabled = false`, `max_review_attempts = 3`, `max_remediation_attempts = 2`, `max_total_tokens/cost/duration = 0`, `allow_isolated_same_model_fallback = false`, `max_package_bytes = 1000000`, `max_package_tokens = 250000`, `max_evidence_bytes = 16000000`, `max_verification_log_bytes = 256000`) with fields `implementation_agent: ReviewAgentConfig`, `reviewer_agent: ReviewAgentConfig` (`review.rs:47-53`: `adapter_id: String, agent_id: String, provider: Option<String>, model: Option<String>`), and `tier_policy: Option<ReviewTierPolicyConfig>`. Legacy alternative: `Config.agents: Option<AgentsConfig>` (`config/mod.rs:102-103`) with `[agents.implementation]`/`[agents.reviewer]` tables (`config/default.toml:193-209`), mutually exclusive with the adapter-neutral `worker_registry`.

