### 2. Onboarding — `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/cli/onboard.rs` (thin CLI) + `crates/familiar-ai-core/src/onboarding.rs` (real logic)

`OnboardCommand` (cli/onboard.rs:11):
```rust
Propose { repository: PathBuf, #[arg(long, default_value="onboarding-proposal.toml")] output: PathBuf }
Approve { proposal: PathBuf, #[arg(long)] answers: PathBuf, #[arg(long)] actor: String, #[arg(long)] repositories_dir: Option<PathBuf> }
Validate { policy: PathBuf }
Fixture { policy: PathBuf }
```

Core functions in `familiar_ai_core::onboarding` (`crates/familiar-ai-core/src/onboarding.rs`):
- `pub fn propose(repository: &Path) -> Result<OnboardingProposal, String>` (line 60) — pure filesystem sniffing (Cargo.toml→rust/cargo, package.json→javascript/typescript/npm, pyproject.toml→python, go.mod→go, pom.xml/build.gradle→java, plus Makefile/justfile/Dockerfile tool detection, `docs/prds`|`docs/prd`|`prds` PRD-dir candidates, protected paths `.git`/`.github`/`docs/adr`/`docs/contracts`). `authority_granted` is always `false`.
- `pub fn encode_proposal(&OnboardingProposal) -> Result<String,String>` (toml pretty)
- `pub fn approve(proposal: &Path, answers: &Path, actor: &str) -> Result<(String hash, String encoded_policy), String>` (line 151) — requires `actor` starts with `human:` and non-empty; requires proposal's `authority_granted == false`; requires `answers.repository` canonicalizes to the proposal's canonical repository; builds a `RepositoryConfig` from the `OnboardingAnswers` and serializes as `[onboarding]` (`PolicyAttribution`) + `[repositories."<canonical path>"]` TOML, sha256-hashed.
- `pub fn validate_policy(path: &Path) -> Result<PolicyAttribution, String>` (line 199) — re-derives the hash and compares (tamper-evident), requires exactly one repository entry keyed by its own canonical path, `human:` actor, and calls `validate_repository_policy` (profile/prd_metadata_policy parse, active_dir != archived_dir, `review`/`execution_context`/`delivery` all present and `.validate()`d).
- `pub fn safe_fixture(path: &Path) -> Result<String, String>` (line 284) — calls `validate_policy` then prints `"fixture ok: context=isolated review=isolated reporting=ok boundary=validated actor=… sha256=…"`.
- `pub fn default_reference_roots_for_answers() -> Vec<ReferenceRootConfig>` (line 270).

Key types:
```rust
pub struct OnboardingProposal { format_version, repository, languages, build_tools, prd_candidates, protected_path_candidates, verification_candidates: Vec<Vec<String>>, authority_granted: bool }
pub struct OnboardingAnswers { repository, profile, active_dir, archived_dir, prd_metadata_policy, #[serde(default)] reference_roots: Vec<ReferenceRootConfig>, #[serde(default)] risk_vocabulary: Vec<String>, review: ReviewConfig, execution_context: ExecutionContextConfig, delivery: DeliveryConfig }
pub struct PolicyAttribution { format_version, actor, repository, content_sha256 }
```
The **answers TOML** file format (see the core test at `onboarding.rs:322-345` and the CLI parse test in `bin/familiar-ai.rs:735-750`) is a flat TOML with `repository`, `profile`, `active_dir`, `archived_dir`, `prd_metadata_policy`, plus `[review]`, `[execution_context]`, `[delivery]` tables, e.g.:
```toml
repository = "/abs/path/to/repo"
profile = "canonical"
active_dir = "docs/prds"
archived_dir = "docs/prds/done"
prd_metadata_policy = "incremental"

[review]
enabled = false

[execution_context]
hard_ceiling_tokens = 1000

[delivery]
mode = "disabled"
```

CLI `onboard approve` (`cli/onboard.rs:44-101`) resolves `AppPaths`, loads the machine `config.toml`, computes `repositories_dir` (defaults to `<config_dir>/repositories`), writes the approved policy TOML at `<repositories_dir>/<sha256(canonical_repo_path)>.toml` via write-to-`.pending`+`validate_policy`+atomic `rename`. **This directory is exactly what `Config::load` scans as "fragments"** (`crates/familiar-ai-core/src/config/mod.rs:518-577`): it merges every `*.toml` under `repositories_dir` into the effective `Config.repositories` map, rejecting duplicate repository keys across fragments. So onboarding approval is what makes `drive`/`plan`/`run` see a repository's `RepositoryConfig` (profile, active/archived dirs, review, execution_context, delivery) at all — this is the load-bearing link between onboarding and everything else the acceptance test drives.

`RepositoryConfig` itself (`crates/familiar-ai-core/src/config/repository.rs:9-40`): `profile`, `active_dir`, `archived_dir`, `prd_metadata_policy`, `reference_roots: Vec<ReferenceRootConfig>`, `risk_vocabulary: Vec<String>`, `review: Option<ReviewConfig>`, `execution_context: Option<ExecutionContextConfig>`, `delivery: Option<DeliveryConfig>`, `bindings: BTreeMap<String,String>` (machine-local, never in familiar.toml). Methods: `delivery_policy() -> Result<&DeliveryConfig,String>` (line 92), `layout() -> BacklogLayout` (line 97).

No dedicated onboarding integration test file exists in `familiar-ai-daemon/tests/` or `familiar-ai-core/tests/` — all onboarding tests are the `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` at the bottom of `onboarding.rs` (lines 293-360): `discovery_is_structural_and_never_grants_authority`, `approval_is_attributed_hashed_and_tamper_evident`.

