**Worktree lease API** — `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/worktree.rs`:
```rust
pub struct WorktreeOwnership { session_id, prd_id, worktree: PathBuf, created_at, heartbeat_at, state: String }
pub fn recover_incomplete(state_dir: &Path) -> io::Result<usize>          // terminalizes "owned" → "retained_interrupted"; this IS the supervisor-restart recovery path
pub struct WorktreeLease { .. }
impl WorktreeLease {
    pub fn create(repository, state_dir, session_id, prd_id) -> io::Result<Self>
    pub fn create_component(repository, state_dir, session_id, component_id, configured_root) -> io::Result<Self>
    pub fn create_component_at(repository, state_dir, session_id, component_id, configured_root, revision) -> io::Result<Self>
    pub fn path(&self) -> &Path
    pub fn ownership_path(&self) -> &Path
    pub fn branch(&self) -> &str
    pub fn heartbeat(&mut self) -> io::Result<()>
    pub fn mark_retained(&mut self) -> io::Result<()>
    pub fn mark_state(&mut self, state: &str) -> io::Result<()>
    pub fn start_heartbeat(&self, interval) -> WorktreeHeartbeatGuard
}
```
`.ownership.json` files under `<state_dir>/worktrees/<session_id>/<safe_component_id>.ownership.json` are the durable evidence `recover_incomplete` scans for on restart; `state` transitions `"owned"` → `"retained_interrupted"` on recovery (never deletes work). This is the mechanism to simulate "process kill and restart": write/leave an `.ownership.json` with `state: "owned"`, call `worktree::recover_incomplete(state_dir)`, assert it becomes `"retained_interrupted"` and a `<id>.result.json` (`WorktreeRecoveryResult{session_id,prd_id,outcome,reason,recovered_at}`) is written (see `worktree.rs` test `recovery_terminalizes_owned_worktree_and_is_idempotent`, lines 366-391).

**Best reference tests for a fake agent plugged into `drive()`** — `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/drive_loop.rs` (full text read above). Reusable fixture pattern:
```rust
static WORKING_DIRECTORY: Mutex<()> = Mutex::new(());   // serializes tests; drive() reads process cwd
fn fixture(count, chained) -> (TempDir, AppPaths, Config) { .. }   // builds a git repo with N PRDs
fn with_working_directory<T>(repository: &Path, body: impl FnOnce() -> T) -> T { .. } // chdir guard
```
Fake agents shown there: `RecordingAgent` (records the `## PRD: …` marker line from the prompt, returns success), `ConcurrencyAgent` (tracks `active`/`peak` AtomicUsize + `std::thread::sleep(150ms)` to prove bounded parallelism — see `independent_scopes_execute_with_bounded_parallelism`, asserting `agent.peak == 2` when `config.driver.max_concurrency=2; max_parallel_components=2; isolated_worktrees=true`), `PanickingAgent`, `FailingPreflightAgent` (implements `fn preflight(&self) -> Result<(), String>` to fail before any execution — used for `PreflightFailed` termination test).

**End-to-end real completion with real review** (implementation + isolated worktrees + merge-queue integration + backlog completion) — `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/autonomous_delivery.rs` (full text read above): `WritingAgent` (writes `src/prd_N.txt` recording whether `src/prd_1.txt` was already present in its base — proves merge-queue ordering), `CleanReviewer` (reads `review-request.json` from its isolated workspace, echoes `review_id`/`manifest_hash`, `findings: []`). This is the closest existing precedent for the multi-repo acceptance test's "independent branches execute concurrently while dependencies and shared scopes serialize correctly" criterion — it asserts on `OrchestrationRepository::new(db.conn()).integration_revision(session_id)` and `DriverRepository::new(db.conn()).selection_decisions(session_id)` (looking for `"deferred_scope_overlap"`/`"deferred_scope_held"` then `"ready_selected"`).

Worker routing (cheap/local vs strong-review) machinery lives in `run.rs`:
```rust
pub struct RouteContext { pub risk_classes: Vec<String>, pub expected_file_count: u64 }
pub fn resolved_worker_plan(config: &Config, route_context: &RouteContext) -> Result<(AgentEntryConfig, AgentEntryConfig, Vec<SelectionRecord>), String>
pub fn next_implementation_worker(config: &Config, route_context: &RouteContext) -> Result<Option<(String, AgentEntryConfig)>, String>
```
driven by `WorkerRegistryConfig`/`WorkerRoutingConfig`/`WorkerRouteRuleConfig` (`crates/familiar-ai-core/src/config/registry_workers.rs:264-427`), e.g. rules keyed by `max_expected_files`/`risk_classes` picking between named `RegistryWorkerConfig` entries with `capabilities: Vec<WorkerCapabilityConfig>` (`Planning|Implementation|Review|Remediation|NarrowTask`). `drive_loop.rs`'s `legacy_prd_document_scope_routes_and_persists_the_derived_file_count` test (lines 305-398) is the concrete worked example: it builds a fake `codex`-protocol executable script, registers two `RegistryWorkerConfig`s ("small"/"large") with route rules `max_expected_files: 1` vs `3`, and asserts the selection lands in the `worker_selections` SQL table (`rule='user-pin'`, `selected_identity LIKE 'wspec-sha256:%'`, `expected_file_count=3`).

