### 8. Existing test files under `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/`

Full list (24 files): `agents.rs`, `autonomous_delivery.rs`, `cli_bootstrap.rs`, `cli_next.rs`, `cli_record_complete.rs`, `cli_recovery.rs`, `cli_run.rs`, `cli_stewardship.rs`, `context_service.rs`, `control_plane_boundaries.rs`, `credential_store.rs`, `drive_loop.rs`, `driver_hygiene.rs`, `familiar_toml.rs`, `identity_continuity.rs`, `integration.rs`, `preflight_contract.rs`, `registry_migration.rs`, `review_routing.rs`, `security_burn_in.rs`, `stewardship_cross_surface.rs`, `token_compression.rs`, `verification_truth.rs`.

Most relevant to PRD-038, in priority order (all read in full above except review_routing.rs, which was less relevant):
1. **`drive_loop.rs`** (653 lines) — PRD-020-equivalent parallel-driver reference: warrant refusal, attempt loop + `DeterministicFailureCascade`, worker routing selection persisted in `worker_selections` SQL table, `prd_count_ceiling`, dependency-blocking (`a_retained_prd_makes_its_dependents_ineligible`), empty backlog, cost-unknown-ceiling, **`independent_scopes_execute_with_bounded_parallelism`** (the direct PRD-020 concurrency proof via `ConcurrencyAgent` peak-count), panicked-worker handling, preflight-failure handling. Uses a process-wide `static WORKING_DIRECTORY: Mutex<()>` because `drive()` reads `std::env::current_dir()`.
2. **`autonomous_delivery.rs`** (340 lines) — real end-to-end `drive()` completion with actual structured review (`WritingAgent`+`CleanReviewer`), asserting merge-queue integration order and the deterministic-failure circuit breaker (three identical retained-outcomes trip `DeterministicFailureCascade` with an actionable recovery-plan `termination_detail`).
3. **`security_burn_in.rs`** (325 lines) — PRD-037-equivalent failure-injection/burn-in: `CommandRunner` fault injection into delivery, journal corruption, ambiguous external-effect reconciliation, systemd/launchd restart-throttle and injection-safety, secret-redaction across report/db/PR-comment surfaces, plus a meta-test (`coverage_matrix_identifiers_resolve_to_collected_tests`) that cross-checks `docs/security/coverage-matrix.md` identifiers against actual `fn` names in `crates/` — worth checking if PRD-038 needs a coverage-matrix entry too.
4. **`driver_hygiene.rs`** (184 lines) — attempt-local context-failure blast-radius isolation (one PRD's missing reference doesn't fail the whole session) and CLI subprocess `backlog metadata-check --advisory/--strict` exit-code contract.
5. **`cli_recovery.rs`** / **`cli_bootstrap.rs`** — canonical subprocess-invocation patterns (`Command::new(env!("CARGO_BIN_EXE_familiar-ai"))` + `FAMILIAR_AI_DATABASE__PATH`/`XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` env, exact stdout/stderr byte assertions).
6. No dedicated PRD-039 ("resume all concurrent") test file was found under `tests/`; concurrent resume logic and its `std::thread::scope`-based wave execution lives directly in `src/resume.rs::execute_configured` (read in full above, lines 1-246) — only unit-testable indirectly or via CLI subprocess (`familiar-ai resume all`). No PRD-036 onboarding integration test file exists either — onboarding is only unit-tested inside `familiar-ai-core/src/onboarding.rs`.

**Common test-helper patterns observed across files:**
- Temp git repos: `tempfile::tempdir()`, then a small `fn git(repository: &Path, args: &[&str])` helper running `Command::new("git")` with `GIT_AUTHOR_NAME`/`EMAIL`/`GIT_COMMITTER_NAME`/`EMAIL` env set (avoids relying on global git config) — repeated verbatim in `drive_loop.rs`, `autonomous_delivery.rs`, `driver_hygiene.rs`.
- Building a `familiar_ai_core::AppPaths` by hand under a temp dir root (`config_dir/data_dir/state_dir/runtime_dir/log_dir/socket_path/pid_path`) rather than calling `AppPaths::resolve()`, when calling library functions (`drive()`, `deliver_with()`, etc.) directly in-process.
- SQLite session DB setup: either `Database::open_in_memory()` + `.run_migrations()` for pure-storage/report tests, or `Database::open(&paths.data_dir.join("familiar.db"))` set via `config.database.path = Some(...)` for `drive()`-driven tests (the driver opens/migrates it itself).
- In-process vs subprocess CLI invocation: **library-level** tests (`drive_loop.rs`, `autonomous_delivery.rs`, `driver_hygiene.rs`) call `familiar_ai_daemon::drive::drive(...)` directly with fake `CodingAgent`s and a manually `chdir`'d process (needed because `drive()`/`deliver()`/`resume()`/`plan()` all resolve `std::env::current_dir()` internally) — this is faster and lets you inject fakes. **Subprocess** tests (`cli_recovery.rs`, `cli_bootstrap.rs`, `driver_hygiene.rs`'s CLI test) spawn the real `familiar-ai` binary via `env!("CARGO_BIN_EXE_familiar-ai")` — needed to exercise the actual `onboard`/`worker install`/exit-code contracts, but cannot inject fake agents (would need a fake executable script on `$PATH`/registered as a worker, as done in `drive_loop.rs`'s codex-protocol shell-script worker).
- Executable-script fake worker pattern (`drive_loop.rs:319-323`): write a `#!/bin/sh` script honoring `--version` and reading/writing the codex JSONL wire protocol (`{"type":"turn.completed"}`), `chmod 0o755`, and register it as a `RegistryWorkerConfig{ adapter: Some(AgentAdapterKind::Codex), executable: Some(path), ... }` — this is how you'd drive a *subprocess*-invoked `familiar-ai` binary with deterministic fake agent behavior when an in-process `CodingAgent` fake isn't available (subprocess tests can't share Rust trait objects across the process boundary).

