## 4. `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/` — PRD-056 application services, PRD-039 lifecycle, PRD-024/064 warrants, MCP capability sessions, worker launch pattern

**Application-service / capability-scoped session layer (PRD-056)** — the closest existing analog to what PRD-058 calls "MCP capability-scoped sessions":
- **`control_plane.rs`** (914 lines) — `ControlPlaneService` — "Transport-free application boundary. CLI, socket, dashboard and MCP are adapters; authorization and mutation are centralized here." Methods: `register_project`, `set_project_state`, `submit`, `observe`, `claim_next`, `recover`, `reconcile_filesystem`, and more (progress/evidence/escalation, not fully shown but implied by `Authority` variants). Every mutating/observing call takes a `&CapabilityScope` and calls `require(scope, Authority::X, project, execution)` (line 469) which checks: authority is granted, project matches scope's `project_id` (if scoped), execution matches scope's `execution_id` (if scoped) — **this `require()` function is the authorization-gate pattern PRD-058 should reuse/extend for its own tool-capability authorization** (authority scope + project/execution binding, exactly what the PRD's "Authorize" stage needs).
- **`crates/familiar-ai-core/src/control_plane.rs`** — the shared types: `Authority` enum (`Observe, Control, ReadWarrant, ReadAccountingLabels, ReportProgress, SubmitEvidence, RequestEscalation` — **this is literally the canonical-tool-capability-adjacent authority vocabulary**, already includes `ReportProgress`/`SubmitEvidence`/`RequestEscalation` which map directly to 3 of PRD-058's canonical tool capabilities), `CapabilityScope { client_class, project_id: Option, execution_id: Option, attempt: Option, worker_id: Option, authorities: Vec<Authority> }`, `CapabilityGrant { credential, scope, expires_at }`, `AgentCapabilityView { project_id, execution_id, attempt, worker_id, state, warrant_json, remaining_reservations_json }`, `OwnershipClaim`, `SchedulingPolicy`, `Submission`/`ExecutionRecord`/`ControlEvent`/`ExecutionState`/`ProjectState`/`ExecutionMode`/`ClientClass`.
- **`local_transport.rs`** — Unix-domain-socket transport for the above: `ClientHello`/`ServerHello` handshake (`negotiate`), `require_same_user` (peer-credential check), `ControlRequest`/`ControlResponse` enums (Hello/Health/RegisterProject/SetProjectState/Submit/Observe/Execution/RequestEscalation/Cancel/AgentView/ReportProgress/SubmitEvidence), `LocalHost` (bind/serve), `LocalClient`, `MutationOwner`/`resolve_mutation_owner`. Confirms `session_reference`/`owner_nonce` in `ClientHello` are explicitly documented as "must never be included in a model-facing response, prompt, log, or accounting row" — a concrete precedent for PRD-058's "no billing/admin credentials in tool env" requirement.
- **`crates/familiar-ai-mcp/src/tools/control_plane.rs`** — projects exactly 4 capability-scoped MCP tools onto this: `control.get_assignment` (View), `control.report_progress` (Progress), `control.submit_evidence` (Evidence), `control.request_escalation` (Escalate) — **this is a working, minimal example of "one capability, N projections" for 3 of PRD-058's canonical capabilities** (report-progress, submit-evidence, request-escalation), talking to the daemon over the `LocalClient`/`local_transport` socket. New canonical tool capabilities (read-file, search/list, run-command, apply-edit) would extend this pattern with new `Operation` variants and new `ControlRequest` cases, or a parallel tool set.
- **`control_worker.rs`** — `run(...)` async worker loop, `CommandSpec`, `process_start_identity(pid)` (liveness fingerprinting used with `OwnershipClaim`), `verified_live_workers(...)`.

**Worker launch/supervision pattern (for a new raw-runtime worker type to plug in)**:
- **`run.rs`** (4378 lines, the largest and most central file) — the actual execution engine:
  - `build_agent(entry: &AgentEntryConfig) -> Box<dyn CodingAgent>` — constructs a harness agent from legacy config.
  - `worker_descriptor(...)`, `build_selected_agents(...)`, `resolved_worker_plan(...)`, `next_implementation_worker(...)`, `route_context_for_prd(...)` — build `WorkerDescriptor`s and route through `familiar_ai_agent::registry::WorkerRegistry` (area 1) to pick a worker, then call `AdapterFactories::build()`.
  - `execute_with_config` → `execute_with_config_tracked` → `..._tracked_from` → `..._tracked_from_preflighted` → `..._tracked_from_preflighted_with_route_context[_and_timeout]` → `execute_tracked_inner` (line 1073, the actual subprocess-execute + accounting-persist core) — **this chain is exactly where a raw-runtime execution path would need a parallel/alternate branch**, since today every one of these functions ultimately calls a blocking `CodingAgent::execute()`.
  - `resume_implemented_checkpoint(...)` (line 809) — **the existing PRD-039 checkpoint/resume mechanism** for implementation-stage work; relevant precedent for PRD-058's "loop checkpoint (conversation position, journal high-water mark, usage so far)."
  - `run_review(...)` (2252) with `ReviewRunInput`, `compute_review_preflight`, `build_scope_policy(...)` (2070, wires `familiar-ai-review`'s `ScopePolicyInput`/`compile_scope_policy`), `map_file_class`/`map_class_policy`/`map_change_kind` — bridges config `ScopeFileClassName`/`ScopeClassPolicyConfig` (area 3) into the review crate's `ScopeFileClass`/`ScopeClassPolicy` (area 7).
  - `persist_accounting_observations(...)` (2770) — writes PRD-051 usage rows; **the precedent for PRD-058's usage-observation persistence**, must be checked closely for the sanitized-envelope pattern before writing new code.
  - `capture_worktree_baseline`, `git_common_directory_evidence`, `calculate_cost`, `terminal(...)`, `finalize(...)` — execution-history finalization helpers.
  - `build_prompt`, `render_prompt[_with_prefix]` — **existing stable-prefix prompt construction** (PRD-029) directly relevant to PRD-058's "byte-stable prefix" compose stage.
- **`drive.rs`** (3532 lines) — the outer unattended drive-session loop (PRD-024 warrant enforcement — "refuses to start unless at least one ceiling is finite"), orchestrates repeated `run.rs` executions across PRDs; owns session-level budget reservations.
- **`supervisor.rs`** (335 lines) — process supervision for the persistent daemon worker (systemd/launchd), separate concern from per-execution agent launch.
- **`billing.rs`/`openai_billing.rs`** — PRD-052/054-era provider billing-report ingestion (authoritative revisions, reconciliation), separate from per-attempt PRD-051 observations recorded in `run.rs`.

**PRD-039 phase machine**: No literal `PhaseMachine`/`WorkflowPhase` type found; the phase discipline is implicit in the `execute_with_config_tracked_*` call chain plus `familiar-ai-storage`'s `lifecycle.rs` repo (`LifecycleChange`, `RetirementReason`, `PendingSummaryWork`, `LifecycleOutcome`, `ScanStatus`, `ScanRun`, `trait LifecycleRepository`) which governs PRD-workflow lifecycle transitions/retirement, not execution-attempt phases per se.

**Plug-in point summary**: A new raw-runtime worker type would most naturally enter at the `WorkerDescriptor`/`AdapterFactory` boundary (area 1) and the `build_agent`/`execute_with_config_tracked_*` chain in `run.rs` — either by making `CodingAgent` accommodate a raw-runtime-driven implementation (adapting the streaming loop to the blocking `execute()` contract), or by introducing a parallel raw-runtime execution path in `familiar-ai-daemon` (PRD's expected `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs`) that still terminates in the same accounting/evidence/review integration points (`persist_accounting_observations`, `run_review`, `finalize`).

