## 7. Sandbox / journal / scope-authorization search (cross-workspace)

Ranked by relevance:

- **`crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/isolation.rs`** — Landlock (Linux)/`sandbox-exec` (macOS) process sandbox + process-group timeout/kill watchdog. (Full detail in area 1.) This is the isolation layer PRD-058 explicitly says to reuse ("the existing isolation layer (Landlock/sandbox-exec, process groups, timeouts) encloses commands"). It currently only supports **denying** one subtree's reads for an isolated reviewer process — it does **not** yet support: confining writes to a worktree, network deny-by-default, or a general allow/deny command policy. All three are new work for PRD-058, built on top of this primitive.
- **`crates/familiar-ai-review/src/policy.rs`** (1668 lines) — **the PRD-013 ScopePolicy engine, directly reusable**:
  - `SCOPE_POLICY_SCHEMA_VERSION = "scope-policy/1"`, `BUILTIN_SCOPE_RULES_VERSION = "builtin-scope-rules/1"`.
  - `ScopePolicyInput`, `ScopePolicyError`, `compile_scope_policy(...) -> ...` (line 66) — builds a deterministic policy snapshot from config (`ScopeFileClassPolicies`, `ScopeClassificationRule`s, `ScopeDeclarationMode`).
  - `ScopeEvidence`, `Classified`, `Subject`, **`evaluate_scope(...)`** (line 348, ~366 lines) — the core evaluator: classifies each changed file, applies declaration mode (expected-file match vs. configured allowlist), applies class policy (allow/human-review), producing `scope_check_result(...)`/`scope_rule_summary(...)`.
  - `BlockingPolicy`, `ReviewValidationError`, `check_independence(...)`.
  - This is exactly the engine PRD-058's "Write-capable tools are bounded by the deterministic scope authorization contract" acceptance criterion should call into for `apply-edit` — **do not reimplement scope matching; call `compile_scope_policy`/`evaluate_scope` with the execution's expected-files declaration.**
- **`crates/familiar-ai-review/src/types.rs`** — the full PRD-013 type vocabulary: `ScopeCheckResult`, `ScopeDisposition`, `ScopeFileClass` (dependency_manifest/lockfile/migration/configuration/test/generated_artifact + general), `ScopeDecision`, `ScopeRuleSource`, `ExpectedFileMatch`, **`ScopeFinding`** (line 457), `ScopeDeclarationMode`, `ScopeClassPolicy`, `ScopeFileClassPolicies`, `ScopePathEntry`, `ProhibitedRuleKind`/`ProhibitedRule`, `ScopeClassificationRule`, `ScopePolicySnapshot`, `ScopeEvaluation`, `ScopeRuleSummary`.
- **`crates/familiar-ai-review/src/tier.rs`/`coordinator.rs`** — reference `ScopePolicy`/`ScopeFinding` in review-tier selection and cycle coordination (grep hit); the coordinator (1987 lines) is where scope evaluation is wired into the review lifecycle end-to-end (`run.rs`'s `build_scope_policy` bridges into this).
- **No write-ahead tool journal exists anywhere** — grep for "journal" across the workspace matched only unrelated hits (agent/daemon/review files that don't implement a journal — the earlier grep on "journal" combined with the other terms produced false-positive file matches from the OR'd pattern, not an actual journal implementation). This confirms PRD-058's write-ahead tool journal (intent-then-result records, side-effect-class-aware resume policy) is **wholly new** — nothing to reuse beyond the general append-only/`no_update`/`no_delete` trigger pattern already used in migrations 030/041/054, and the `Database`/`Connection` transaction idioms in `familiar-ai-storage/src/db.rs` and the repo files above.
- **`crates/familiar-ai-storage/src/db.rs`** and **`repos/stewardship.rs`** matched the grep only for tangential term overlap (e.g. "scope" in an unrelated sense, or transaction/journal-mode SQLite PRAGMA usage) — worth a quick look if you need SQLite's own WAL journal mode config, but not a PRD-013/058 scope or tool-journal implementation.
- **`crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/security_burn_in.rs`/`integration.rs`** — existing sandbox/isolation test precedent (matched "sandbox"/"landlock" grep) — check these for the testing conventions used to validate isolation guarantees; useful template for PRD-058's own sandbox/env-scrubbing tests.

